# Performance Standards

- This is a stack-specific rule file for Node.js runtime behavior.
- Optimize only after identifying the hot path, bottleneck, or operational limit being addressed.
- Keep request and event handlers non-blocking. Avoid CPU-heavy work, large synchronous loops, and blocking I/O on hot paths.
- For large payloads or long-running transfers, prefer streams and backpressure-aware APIs over buffering entire datasets in memory.
- Reuse shared serializers, validators, and transport helpers on hot paths instead of rebuilding equivalent logic per feature.
- Avoid accidental quadratic work in controllers, handlers, serializers, and mappers when processing lists or streams.
- When a change targets performance-sensitive behavior, document the assumption being optimized and verify the result with measurement or profiling.
- Compare the baseline and candidate using the same workload, limits, runtime, environment, warm-up, and concurrency. Record revisions, commands, sample counts, and relevant latency distributions, throughput, memory, or resource measurements so the comparison can be reproduced.
- Repeat measurements sufficiently to distinguish the claimed gain from run-to-run variation; report inconclusive results as inconclusive. Investigate regressions in individual scenarios rather than hiding them in aggregate averages or selecting only favorable runs.
- Keep benchmark acceptance limits unchanged when evaluating an optimization. Run the affected end-to-end REST and Socket benchmarks when claiming a gain on those paths; a microbenchmark alone does not establish end-to-end improvement.
- Performance evidence supplements, rather than replaces, correctness and regression tests required by `testing.mdc`.
